    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.node_percolation - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.node_percolation.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.node_percolation()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Simulate node percolation by randomly removing nodes.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.node_percolation()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Simulate node percolation by randomly removing nodes.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.node_percolation()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Simulate node percolation by randomly removing nodes.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nSimulate node percolation by randomly removing nodes.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L57697-L57824){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.node_percolation(\n    q,\n    seed=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Node percolation](../assets/graphs/node-percolation.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Before: the two diamonds sharing hub D. After removing 30 percent of the nodes at random (here D and F, drawn dashed with their edges), the surviving graph has split into the triangle A, B, C and the pair E, G.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nCreates a new graph with a fraction of nodes removed uniformly at random, along with all\nof their incident edges. This is the basic operation of *site percolation* and models\nrandom failures: servers going down, people being immunized or leaving a social network,\nstations closing in a transport network. Comparing the surviving graph with the original\n(for example, with `largest_component_size()`) shows how well the network holds together\nwhen nodes fail at random.\n\nTypical uses include:\n\n- stress-testing the robustness of a network to random node failures\n- running many randomized trials to estimate how connectivity decays with `q=`\n- generating perturbed versions of a graph for sensitivity analyses\n\nThe number of nodes removed is `round(q * n)`, where `n` is the node count, so the\nremoved fraction is exact up to rounding. Every node is equally likely to be removed;\nfor targeted attacks (removing hubs first), select and delete nodes by a centrality score\ninstead. For a whole sweep over `q=` with averaging, see\n`percolation_threshold_analysis()`.\n\nThe original graph is not modified. The returned graph keeps the directedness of the\noriginal, and surviving nodes and edges keep their IDs, labels, weights and attributes.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[q]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Fraction of nodes to remove, between `0.0` and `1.0` inclusive. For example, `q=0.2`\n    removes 20% of the nodes. `q=0.0` returns a copy of the graph structure and `q=1.0`\n    returns an empty graph.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Seed for the random number generator. Calls with the same graph, `q=`, and `seed=`\n    remove the same nodes, which makes results reproducible. If `None` (default), a fresh\n    random seed is drawn on every call.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new Graph containing only the surviving nodes and the edges between them.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `q=` is outside the range `[0.0, 1.0]`.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~edge_percolation`) \n\n:   Remove edges instead of nodes.\n\n[](`~percolation_threshold_analysis`) \n\n:   Track the largest component over many removal rates.\n\n[](`~largest_component_size`) \n\n:   The size of what holds together.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe graph below is two clusters, `A`, `B`, `C` and `E`, `F`, `G`, held together by the hub\n`D`. Removing 30% of its seven nodes takes out two of them. With this seed, one of them\nis the hub, so the survivors fall apart:\n\n::: {#92e56221 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"D\"), (\"C\", \"D\"),\n     (\"D\", \"E\"), (\"D\", \"F\"), (\"E\", \"F\"), (\"E\", \"G\"), (\"F\", \"G\")]\n)\n\nsurvivors = graph.node_percolation(q=0.3, seed=9)\n\n(\n    survivors.get_node_df()[\"label\"].to_list(),\n    survivors.largest_component_size(),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(['A', 'B', 'E', 'F', 'G'], 3)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Which members leave\n\nRemoving 30% of Zachary's karate club leaves 24 members. Whether they stay in one piece\ndepends less on how many leave than on who. In these eight draws, the largest piece\nkeeps at least 23 of them whenever the instructor (member 0) stays, and at most 20\nwhenever the instructor leaves:\n\n::: {#23256325 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\nclub = ec.karate_club().layout_kamada_kawai()\n\ndraws = []\nfor seed in range(8):\n    survivors = club.node_percolation(q=0.3, seed=seed)\n    draws.append(\n        {\n            \"seed\": seed,\n            \"instructor_stays\": \"0\" in survivors.get_node_df()[\"label\"],\n            \"largest\": survivors.largest_component_size(),\n        }\n    )\n\npl.DataFrame(draws)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (8, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>seed</th><th>instructor_stays</th><th>largest</th></tr><tr><td>i64</td><td>bool</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>true</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>true</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>true</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>false</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>false</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>false</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>true</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>false</td><td>17</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe survivors keep their attributes, including their positions, so the last draw can be\ndrawn in place. Without the instructor, seven members on the instructor's side are cut\noff from the rest:\n\n::: {#962f1618 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nclub.node_percolation(q=0.3, seed=7).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"faction\", label_by=\"label\"\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\">